{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1562750240,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUwOTk3NzcyNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3521#issuecomment-509977725"},"message":"That approach seems to work just fine with symlinking from within the target object tree into the source object tree as well as just symlinking the entire `.git/annex/object`. All it takes to make annex aware is a `git annex fsck` afterwards.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eExploration for symlinking the entire object tree:\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nFirst, set up an original data source to provide some keys:\n```\n/tmp$ datalad create origin\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/origin \ncreate(ok): /tmp/origin (dataset)\n/tmp$ cd origin\n/tmp/origin$ echo \"some\" \u003e file_a\n/tmp/origin$ echo \"other\" \u003e file_b\n/tmp/origin$ datalad save -m \"provide some content\"\nadd(ok): file_a (file)                                                                                                       \nadd(ok): file_b (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\n/tmp/origin$ cd ..\n```\n\nNow, have the actual source dataset (target of the symlink we want to build later on), that'll have a file present and another not:\n```\n/tmp$ datalad install -s origin source\n[INFO   ] Cloning origin into '/tmp/source' \ninstall(ok): /tmp/source (dataset)\n/tmp$ cd source\n/tmp/source$ datalad get file_a\nget(ok): /tmp/source/file_a (file) [from origin...]\n/tmp/source$ cd ..\n```\nFinally build our test dataset: \n```\n/tmp$ datalad install -s source test\n[INFO   ] Cloning source into '/tmp/test' \ninstall(ok): /tmp/test (dataset)\n/tmp$ cd test\n/tmp/test$ ln -s /tmp/source/.git/annex/objects .git/annex/objects\n/tmp/test$ tree .git/annex\n.git/annex\n├── index\n├── index.lck\n├── journal\n├── journal.lck\n├── mergedrefs\n├── objects -\u003e /tmp/source/.git/annex/objects\n├── othertmp.lck\n├── sentinal\n└── sentinal.cache\n\n2 directories, 7 files\n/tmp/test$ git annex whereis file_a\nwhereis file_a (2 copies) \n        b66ae28f-6d1f-4821-a20a-da5c739ace20 -- ben@tree:/tmp/source [origin]\n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin\nok\n/tmp/test$ git annex whereis file_b\nwhereis file_b (1 copy) \n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin\nok\n```\nAnd make annex aware:\n```\n/tmp/test$ git annex fsck\nfsck file_a (fixing location log) (checksum...) ok\nfsck file_b ok\n(recording state in git...)\n/tmp/test$ tree .git/annex\n.git/annex\n├── index\n├── index.lck\n├── journal\n├── journal.lck\n├── mergedrefs\n├── objects -\u003e /tmp/source/.git/annex/objects\n├── othertmp.lck\n├── sentinal\n└── sentinal.cache\n\n2 directories, 7 files\n/tmp/test$ git annex whereis file_a\nwhereis file_a (3 copies) \n        96b10e95-378f-4ab3-9932-86d9c265268d -- ben@tree:/tmp/test [here]\n        b66ae28f-6d1f-4821-a20a-da5c739ace20 -- ben@tree:/tmp/source [origin]\n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin\nok\n/tmp/test$ git annex whereis file_b\nwhereis file_b (1 copy) \n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin\nok\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1562750240,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDI0NzMwMjY3Mw=="},"target":"a584ddf3adac3ae3290136086d73e2285260fd050d3d08954e69b8b7ccd8c375","message":"That approach seems to work just fine with symlinking from within the target object tree into the source object tree as well as just symlinking the entire `.git/annex/object`. All it takes to make annex aware is a `git annex fsck` afterwards.\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eExploration for symlinking the entire object tree:\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nFirst, set up an original data source to provide some keys:\n```\n/tmp$ datalad create origin\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /tmp/origin \ncreate(ok): /tmp/origin (dataset)\n/tmp$ cd origin\n/tmp/origin$ echo \"some\" \u003e file_a\n/tmp/origin$ echo \"other\" \u003e file_b\n/tmp/origin$ datalad save -m \"provide some content\"\nadd(ok): file_a (file)                                                                                                       \nadd(ok): file_b (file)\nsave(ok): . (dataset)\n/tmp/origin$ cd ..\n```\n\nNow, have the actual source dataset (target of the symlink we want to build later on), that'll have a file present and another not:\n```\n/tmp$ datalad install -s origin source\n[INFO   ] Cloning origin into '/tmp/source' \ninstall(ok): /tmp/source (dataset)\n/tmp$ cd source\n/tmp/source$ datalad get file_a\nget(ok): /tmp/source/file_a (file) [from origin...]\n/tmp/source$ cd ..\n```\nFinally build our test dataset: \n```\n/tmp$ datalad install -s source test\n[INFO   ] Cloning source into '/tmp/test' \ninstall(ok): /tmp/test (dataset)\n/tmp$ cd test\n/tmp/test$ ln -s /tmp/source/.git/annex/objects .git/annex/objects\n/tmp/test$ tree .git/annex\n.git/annex\n├── index\n├── index.lck\n├── journal\n├── journal.lck\n├── mergedrefs\n├── objects -\u003e /tmp/source/.git/annex/objects\n├── othertmp.lck\n├── sentinal\n└── sentinal.cache\n\n2 directories, 7 files\n/tmp/test$ git annex whereis file_a\nwhereis file_a (2 copies) \n        b66ae28f-6d1f-4821-a20a-da5c739ace20 -- ben@tree:/tmp/source [origin]\n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin\nok\n/tmp/test$ git annex whereis file_b\nwhereis file_b (1 copy) \n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin\nok\n```\nAnd make annex aware:\n```\n/tmp/test$ git annex fsck\nfsck file_a (fixing location log) (checksum...) ok\nfsck file_b ok\n(recording state in git...)\n/tmp/test$ tree .git/annex\n.git/annex\n├── index\n├── index.lck\n├── journal\n├── journal.lck\n├── mergedrefs\n├── objects -\u003e /tmp/source/.git/annex/objects\n├── othertmp.lck\n├── sentinal\n└── sentinal.cache\n\n2 directories, 7 files\n/tmp/test$ git annex whereis file_a\nwhereis file_a (3 copies) \n        96b10e95-378f-4ab3-9932-86d9c265268d -- ben@tree:/tmp/test [here]\n        b66ae28f-6d1f-4821-a20a-da5c739ace20 -- ben@tree:/tmp/source [origin]\n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin\nok\n/tmp/test$ git annex whereis file_b\nwhereis file_b (1 copy) \n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin\nok\n```\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\nEdit: \n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eFurther examination:\u003c/summary\u003e\n\nLet's get `file_a`, which is already there.\n```\n/tmp/test$ git annex get file_a\n/tmp/test$ tree .git/annex/\n.git/annex/\n├── index\n├── index.lck\n├── journal\n├── journal.lck\n├── mergedrefs\n├── objects -\u003e /tmp/source/.git/annex/objects\n├── othertmp.lck\n├── sentinal\n└── sentinal.cache\n\n2 directories, 7 files\n```\nAll good - doesn't mess up things.\nNow, what if we make `origin` known and thereby provide a source for `file_b`?\n\n```\n/tmp/test$ git remote add original ../origin\n/tmp/test$ git annex get file_b\nget file_b (from original...) (checksum...) ok\n(recording state in git...)\n/tmp/test$ tree .git/annex\n.git/annex\n├── index\n├── index.lck\n├── journal\n├── journal.lck\n├── mergedrefs\n├── objects -\u003e /tmp/source/.git/annex/objects\n├── othertmp.lck\n├── sentinal\n├── sentinal.cache\n├── tmp\n└── transfer\n    └── download\n        └── dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463\n\n6 directories, 7 files\n/tmp/test$ git annex whereis file_b\nwhereis file_b (2 copies) \n        96b10e95-378f-4ab3-9932-86d9c265268d -- ben@tree:/tmp/test [here]\n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin [original]\nok\n```\nLGTM. What about source now?\n\n```\n/tmp/source$ git annex whereis file_b\nwhereis file_b (1 copy) \n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin [origin]\nok\n/tmp/source$ git annex fsck\nfsck file_a (checksum...) ok\nfsck file_b (fixing location log) (checksum...) ok\n(recording state in git...)\n/tmp/source$ git annex whereis file_b\nwhereis file_b (2 copies) \n        b66ae28f-6d1f-4821-a20a-da5c739ace20 -- ben@tree:/tmp/source [here]\n        dc8f097e-e918-4567-9b62-0407b1ba6463 -- ben@tree:/tmp/origin [origin]\nok\n```\nAlso good. The need for an `fsck` in order to source be aware of the presence of `file_b` is to be expected.\n\u003c/details\u003e","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1562755865,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI0NzIxNTE4ODI="},"added":["severity-wishlist"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1562757666,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUxMDAyMDQ4Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3521#issuecomment-510020486"},"message":"FTR: Using `git annex fsck --fast` instead seems to be the better approach, of course, and that works just as well in the examples above.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1562809353,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUxMDI5MjY5OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3521#issuecomment-510292699"},"message":"Symlinking .git/annex will work. Symlinking only .git/annex/objects will cause\ngit-annex to be unusually slow in some situations when it is unable to\nrename() from .git/annex to there.\n\nSymlinking individual object files has been proposed before at some\npoint. IIRC my problem with it was it seemed likely to complicate a\nlarge number of edge cases.\n\n-- \nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1562809381,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUxMDI5Mjc5NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3521#issuecomment-510292795"},"message":"Might a git-worktree(1) rather than a clone be a better way to approach\nthis?\n\n-- \nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1563171024,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUxMTI4MzQ2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3521#issuecomment-511283465"},"message":"@joeyh Thanks for the feedback!\n\nA worktree would be the optimal choice IMHO. However, IIRC GitPython had some issues with that -- although that might be outdated knowledge by now. So until we have managed to complete a relatively substantial RF to get it out of datalad, we are somewhat limited in what we can do (https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2879).\n\nGiven that we have identified one immediately workable path, and a potential future solution, I think we can close this issue. I have created https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3528 with a more focused scope.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1563171024,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDI0OTIyMjU0NQ=="},"target":"29351964a832f31d579057200f76f258594b764c35ca171a87623048f80fd598","message":"@joeyh Thanks for the feedback!\n\nA worktree would be the optimal choice IMHO. However, IIRC GitPython had some issues with that https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/753 -- although that might be outdated knowledge by now. So until we have managed to complete a relatively substantial RF to get it out of datalad, we are somewhat limited in what we can do (https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2879).\n\nGiven that we have identified one immediately workable path, and a potential future solution, I think we can close this issue. I have created https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3528 with a more focused scope.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1563197993,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MjQ4MjkzNTI5Mw=="},"status":2}]}